{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>The wasting of hunger.</b> Onkelos rendered ״נְפִיחֵי כָפָן״, and I have no corroborating proof for it. And from the name of Rabbi Moshe HaDarshan of Toulouse I heard: שְׂעִירֵי רָעָב — a man emaciated grows hair on his flesh." "<b>מזי.</b> An Aramaic term for hair — מִזַיָּא, ״דַּהֲוָה מְהַפֵּךְ בְּמִזַיָּא״ (Megillah 18)." "<b>And the bolts of Reshef.</b> The demons waged war against them, as it says ״וּבְנֵי רֶשֶׁף יַגְבִּיהוּ עוּף״ (Job 5) — and they are demons." "<b>And Qetev of Meriri.</b> And the cutting of a demon whose name is Meriri. <b>Qetev</b> — cutting, as in ״אֱהִי קָטָבְךָ שְׁאוֹל״ (Hosea 13)." "<b>And the tooth of beasts.</b> An incident occurred and the ewes were biting and killing (Sifrei)." "<b>The venom of crawlers of dust.</b> The venom of snakes that move on their bellies upon the dust like water that flows along the ground. זְחִילָה is a term for the flow of water upon the dust, and likewise for the flow of anything that drags along the dust and moves."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Rashi cites Onkelos's Aramaic translation then notes he has no supporting proof; the alternative from Rabbi Moshe HaDarshan reads מזי as related to שֵׂעָר (hair)."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:49:26.082713713Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}